  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> Differentiation gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. If k=0, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> has just one distinct real zero, so exclude that case.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> For any nonzero k, 0 lies between the other two stationary x-values. The derivative changes from positive to negative there, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> is the local maximum. With both ends of the quartic tending upwards, four distinct roots require this maximum to be strictly positive. Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> The stationary points are now ordered <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. Their heights are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>8</mn><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. The two outer stationary points are minima.</p><p><strong>4.</strong> Four distinct crossings occur exactly when both minima are strictly below zero and the middle maximum is above zero. For positive k, the two minimum inequalities become <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>. The first is stronger.</p><p><strong>5.</strong> Therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mroot><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></mroot></mrow></math>, answer A. At equality one minimum touches the axis, merging two roots, so the endpoint is excluded.</p>"
